.page ps 1 list processes

# SYNOPSIS
*ps* [*-fuwaxeA*]

# DESCRIPTION
`ps` reads `/proc` and prints a table of the processes, sorted by process id. All processes are always listed, whichever options are given. The state column is `R` (running or ready), `S` (sleeping or waiting) or `Z` (finished, waiting to be collected). TIME is the CPU time used, as minutes and seconds.

The default columns are PID, USER, state, TIME and the command name.

# OPTIONS
@ *-f*
    Full format: UID, PID, PPID, state, TIME and the whole command line.
@ *u*, *-u*
    User format: adds VSZ and RSS (virtual and resident size in KiB).
@ *w*, *-w*
    Wide: do not shorten the command to fit the terminal.
@ *a*, *x*, *e*, *A*
    Accepted for compatibility and ignored.

The letters may be written with or without a leading dash and combined: `ps aux`, `ps -ef`.

# EXAMPLES
    ps
    ps aux
    ps -ef | grep gapsh

# SEE ALSO
top(1), kill(1), pidof(1), pkill(1)
